WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.360 --> 00:00:07.360
[APPLAUSE]

00:00:08.640 --> 00:00:10.635
JEFF HOWELL: Cool,
thank you everyone.

00:00:10.635 --> 00:00:12.260
So, yeah, we're going
to talk about one

00:00:12.260 --> 00:00:15.260
of my favorite topics,
which is retention.

00:00:15.260 --> 00:00:19.670
And more importantly,
I would say engagement.

00:00:19.670 --> 00:00:22.810
So maybe we'll just
start, as a quick preface,

00:00:22.810 --> 00:00:24.185
I'll just use this
cool Jamboard.

00:00:24.185 --> 00:00:25.434
I'm excited to use it on here.

00:00:32.724 --> 00:00:34.640
So, you know, we've all
launched mobile games.

00:00:34.640 --> 00:00:38.044
And there's sort of two
states that you can kind of

00:00:38.044 --> 00:00:38.960
think of the world at.

00:00:38.960 --> 00:00:40.959
You know, if this is just
sort of your revenue--

00:00:45.680 --> 00:00:48.050
--revenue-over-time
type of thing.

00:00:48.050 --> 00:00:50.409
So, what you unfortunately
see, a lot of games,

00:00:50.409 --> 00:00:51.950
they sort of launch
like this, where,

00:00:51.950 --> 00:00:53.840
you know, there's kind
of a bit of a spike,

00:00:53.840 --> 00:00:54.830
and then they all fall down.

00:00:54.830 --> 00:00:57.371
And then the first game update
comes, there's a little spike,

00:00:57.371 --> 00:01:01.250
and then they sort of tail off.

00:01:01.250 --> 00:01:02.930
And then you see
other games, that are

00:01:02.930 --> 00:01:04.910
sort of up and to the right.

00:01:04.910 --> 00:01:07.550
And, you know, the
difference between the two

00:01:07.550 --> 00:01:10.450
is kind of just a short-term
retention problem.

00:01:10.450 --> 00:01:13.822
These games that we call shark
finning, you know, they're sad.

00:01:13.822 --> 00:01:15.530
But it's really a game
that just does not

00:01:15.530 --> 00:01:18.171
have short-term retention.

00:01:18.171 --> 00:01:19.670
So when you launch
a game like this,

00:01:19.670 --> 00:01:21.560
the real question is: what
does your long-term retention

00:01:21.560 --> 00:01:21.950
look like?

00:01:21.950 --> 00:01:23.750
And that's the thing that we're
going to try to figure out

00:01:23.750 --> 00:01:24.672
today.

00:01:24.672 --> 00:01:25.880
So if I just clear this here.

00:01:25.880 --> 00:01:28.532
So, the interesting
thing, when you

00:01:28.532 --> 00:01:29.990
think about long-term
retention is,

00:01:29.990 --> 00:01:33.110
what does that curve look like?

00:01:33.110 --> 00:01:36.470
Does your curve kind of do
this, and continue to grow?

00:01:36.470 --> 00:01:38.780
Does it sort of flatten out?

00:01:38.780 --> 00:01:41.560
Or does it sort of die
off at a certain point?

00:01:41.560 --> 00:01:43.310
And when you're trying
to plan a business,

00:01:43.310 --> 00:01:45.643
from a business point of view,
this is really important,

00:01:45.643 --> 00:01:49.482
because unfortunately, if
it's sort of the latter,

00:01:49.482 --> 00:01:51.440
you end up with this,
what we call pie-plating.

00:01:51.440 --> 00:01:53.437
So you end up launching
a game, and then it

00:01:53.437 --> 00:01:55.520
kind of makes money, and
then it kind of dies off.

00:01:55.520 --> 00:01:59.210
And then, hopefully you
launch another game in time,

00:01:59.210 --> 00:02:01.760
so you sort of can
catch it again.

00:02:01.760 --> 00:02:05.250
And so you're in
this horrible mode

00:02:05.250 --> 00:02:07.880
of where you're trying to
continue to release product

00:02:07.880 --> 00:02:10.400
just to keep your revenue flat.

00:02:10.400 --> 00:02:12.140
Which, what you really
want is something

00:02:12.140 --> 00:02:15.191
that looks like this, where you
launch one game, and it grows.

00:02:15.191 --> 00:02:16.940
And then the next game
grows on top of it,

00:02:16.940 --> 00:02:18.950
and you get this
sort of stacking.

00:02:18.950 --> 00:02:21.140
And to do this, you really
need to understand what

00:02:21.140 --> 00:02:24.410
long-term retention looks like.

00:02:24.410 --> 00:02:28.310
So I said, most people fall
into this retention model

00:02:28.310 --> 00:02:30.860
where it's
cohorted-based retention,

00:02:30.860 --> 00:02:33.950
where everybody installs on D0.

00:02:33.950 --> 00:02:36.350
If you come back on the
next day after that,

00:02:36.350 --> 00:02:40.107
you're a D1 player,
and so on and so on.

00:02:40.107 --> 00:02:41.690
And you kind of build
a graph of this.

00:02:41.690 --> 00:02:42.920
And like I said, really
what does it look like?

00:02:42.920 --> 00:02:44.690
Because you can't
really extrapolate these

00:02:44.690 --> 00:02:45.398
graphs very well.

00:02:45.398 --> 00:02:47.720
You don't know where
it's going to fall off.

00:02:47.720 --> 00:02:49.550
Is it going to level
off at some point?

00:02:49.550 --> 00:02:53.312
You know, what is a
good number for day 365?

00:02:53.312 --> 00:02:54.020
Who knows, right.

00:02:54.020 --> 00:02:57.480
It's really hard to extract
long-term retention out

00:02:57.480 --> 00:02:57.980
of this.

00:03:00.950 --> 00:03:03.357
So what most people end up
doing is they segment, right.

00:03:03.357 --> 00:03:04.940
So they fall into
this trap here where

00:03:04.940 --> 00:03:07.070
they go well, customers
are important,

00:03:07.070 --> 00:03:09.780
so we're going to build like
a customer-based funnel.

00:03:09.780 --> 00:03:12.542
So, you start off at the top
where people install the game,

00:03:12.542 --> 00:03:14.000
and then there
might be an acquire,

00:03:14.000 --> 00:03:15.584
they might have
finished the tutorial.

00:03:15.584 --> 00:03:17.333
At some point they
come back the next day,

00:03:17.333 --> 00:03:18.620
and they become a player.

00:03:18.620 --> 00:03:19.640
Maybe they put
money into the game,

00:03:19.640 --> 00:03:21.431
and they give you--
they're now a customer.

00:03:21.431 --> 00:03:23.930
And they put more money in the
game, and you become a VIP.

00:03:23.930 --> 00:03:26.450
And then, there's some
sort of perfect customer

00:03:26.450 --> 00:03:29.270
that you're really looking for.

00:03:29.270 --> 00:03:31.880
The real problem about
this is that these are not

00:03:31.880 --> 00:03:32.850
service metrics.

00:03:32.850 --> 00:03:34.190
These are retail metrics.

00:03:34.190 --> 00:03:38.990
So, it's very akin to
old-school AAA, right.

00:03:38.990 --> 00:03:41.230
The player is the person
that played the demo.

00:03:41.230 --> 00:03:43.605
The customer is the person
that actually bought the game.

00:03:43.605 --> 00:03:45.230
And then your VIP
is your collector.

00:03:45.230 --> 00:03:47.229
He's the person who's got
the collector edition.

00:03:47.229 --> 00:03:48.352
They got the DLC.

00:03:48.352 --> 00:03:49.937
But mobile games are not this.

00:03:49.937 --> 00:03:51.770
You know, mobile games
are a service, right.

00:03:51.770 --> 00:03:55.050
And service metrics
are over time.

00:03:55.050 --> 00:03:56.306
So it's really a lot--

00:03:56.306 --> 00:03:57.680
I like using the bar example.

00:03:57.680 --> 00:04:00.590
So, if I was running
Cheers, my primary interest

00:04:00.590 --> 00:04:04.830
would not be how many people
bought a drink one night,

00:04:04.830 --> 00:04:05.330
right.

00:04:05.330 --> 00:04:06.955
It really is, how
many people come back

00:04:06.955 --> 00:04:09.020
every single day, or
every single time,

00:04:09.020 --> 00:04:11.460
and continue-- your repeat
customers, or your regulars,

00:04:11.460 --> 00:04:12.020
right.

00:04:12.020 --> 00:04:16.220
So we launched Contest of
Champions back in 2014,

00:04:16.220 --> 00:04:19.709
and it's been our
strongest game ever.

00:04:19.709 --> 00:04:22.520
It's gotten
phenomenal retention.

00:04:22.520 --> 00:04:25.310
And what we started looking at,
was we tried to break that down

00:04:25.310 --> 00:04:27.710
and figure out why this
retention was so good.

00:04:27.710 --> 00:04:31.190
So the first thing we did is
we broke it up by how many--

00:04:31.190 --> 00:04:33.350
metrics broken up by the
number of days of the week

00:04:33.350 --> 00:04:35.720
that people play.

00:04:35.720 --> 00:04:40.610
And what we found is, the people
that play seven days a week

00:04:40.610 --> 00:04:42.410
look nothing like everyone else.

00:04:42.410 --> 00:04:44.055
So, just in a matter
of engagement,

00:04:44.055 --> 00:04:45.430
the number of
battles people play

00:04:45.430 --> 00:04:48.800
a day, from a seven-day player,
is about two to three times

00:04:48.800 --> 00:04:50.230
more than a six-day player.

00:04:50.230 --> 00:04:52.229
And in fact, the six-day
player looks a lot more

00:04:52.229 --> 00:04:54.985
like the five-day player than
it does the seven-day player.

00:04:54.985 --> 00:04:57.110
And this also works for
monetization, too, as well.

00:04:57.110 --> 00:04:59.659
If you sort of normalize it
off the one-day-a-week player,

00:04:59.659 --> 00:05:01.700
as you'll see, the
seven-day-a-week players spend

00:05:01.700 --> 00:05:05.902
almost five times more than
your one-day-a-week player.

00:05:05.902 --> 00:05:07.860
So we said, I think we're
on to something here.

00:05:07.860 --> 00:05:10.670
So what we ended up doing
is we looked at lapse rate.

00:05:10.670 --> 00:05:14.340
So here's four different
games that we did.

00:05:14.340 --> 00:05:16.400
They're all different genres.

00:05:16.400 --> 00:05:18.560
They're all different
levels of success.

00:05:18.560 --> 00:05:21.230
And we said, what is
the chance that someone

00:05:21.230 --> 00:05:25.160
is going to churn the next week
after playing all seven days?

00:05:25.160 --> 00:05:27.770
And what you realize
is, basically, zero.

00:05:27.770 --> 00:05:29.720
I think on Marvel,
it's like 0.3 percent.

00:05:29.720 --> 00:05:33.400
So it's really, really stable.

00:05:33.400 --> 00:05:35.506
And it looks the exact
same for all these games.

00:05:35.506 --> 00:05:36.880
They all look
very, very similar.

00:05:36.880 --> 00:05:41.170
It's basically what we
call physics for retention.

00:05:41.170 --> 00:05:44.590
So that gave this
concept of regulars.

00:05:44.590 --> 00:05:47.850
So we say a regular
Kabam is somebody who has

00:05:47.850 --> 00:05:49.666
played seven days consecutive.

00:05:49.666 --> 00:05:51.790
And then, that's on a daily,
and then on a monthly,

00:05:51.790 --> 00:05:56.710
it's 90% of the month,
so 28 days out of 31.

00:05:56.710 --> 00:05:59.812
And so, in the regular base
model, it's much different.

00:05:59.812 --> 00:06:01.270
And you can look
at it as a funnel,

00:06:01.270 --> 00:06:03.811
but I think it's much better
off if you kind of draw this out

00:06:03.811 --> 00:06:05.590
here, because that's
more like a state

00:06:05.590 --> 00:06:07.210
machine than an actual funnel.

00:06:11.482 --> 00:06:13.690
So at the end the day, what
you really want to get to

00:06:13.690 --> 00:06:17.139
is your regular customers.

00:06:22.030 --> 00:06:25.550
So, basically everybody
starts off as being a player.

00:06:28.290 --> 00:06:30.360
They play the game,
they install the game.

00:06:30.360 --> 00:06:32.820
Now, there's two ways you can
become a regular customer,

00:06:32.820 --> 00:06:33.320
right.

00:06:33.320 --> 00:06:36.837
So the first one is, you
can put money in the game

00:06:36.837 --> 00:06:37.920
and you can be a customer.

00:06:40.800 --> 00:06:45.664
And then after that, you end up
playing seven days consecutive.

00:06:45.664 --> 00:06:47.080
The other way you
can do it is you

00:06:47.080 --> 00:06:48.246
can go the other way around.

00:06:48.246 --> 00:06:51.600
So you basically,
you're a regular now,

00:06:51.600 --> 00:06:53.510
because you played
seven days, and then you

00:06:53.510 --> 00:06:54.510
put money into the game.

00:06:57.680 --> 00:06:59.210
So that's how you
enter the flow.

00:06:59.210 --> 00:07:00.810
Now, once you're a
regular customer,

00:07:00.810 --> 00:07:02.351
you're not always
a regular customer.

00:07:02.351 --> 00:07:04.670
So for some reason,
you miss a day,

00:07:04.670 --> 00:07:07.242
you kind of go off into
this other pool of being,

00:07:07.242 --> 00:07:08.450
basically, non-regular again.

00:07:11.380 --> 00:07:13.690
We call this being weakened.

00:07:13.690 --> 00:07:15.640
But, if you end up
playing seven days again,

00:07:15.640 --> 00:07:18.280
you can come back into the pool.

00:07:18.280 --> 00:07:21.130
There's also the lapse
people, too, as well.

00:07:21.130 --> 00:07:28.940
So if you end up not
playing at all for 14 days,

00:07:28.940 --> 00:07:30.274
you can fall in this lapse pool.

00:07:30.274 --> 00:07:32.939
But if you ever come back to the
game, you become regular again,

00:07:32.939 --> 00:07:34.760
you can become a
regular customer again.

00:07:34.760 --> 00:07:37.093
So this is really interesting
when you view it this way,

00:07:37.093 --> 00:07:39.020
because when you think
about your business,

00:07:39.020 --> 00:07:41.269
each one of these groups
need different things, right.

00:07:41.269 --> 00:07:44.000
So a player that comes
in and gives you money,

00:07:44.000 --> 00:07:45.500
now you try to
figure out how to get

00:07:45.500 --> 00:07:46.797
them to play seven days a week.

00:07:46.797 --> 00:07:49.130
These guys, they're obviously
playing seven days a week,

00:07:49.130 --> 00:07:50.644
how do you then convert them?

00:07:50.644 --> 00:07:52.060
For people that
have weakened, you

00:07:52.060 --> 00:07:53.780
try to build game
designs and game

00:07:53.780 --> 00:07:56.534
systems that are going to bring
them back seven days a week.

00:07:56.534 --> 00:07:57.950
For people who
have lapsed, you're

00:07:57.950 --> 00:07:59.660
going to try to reactivate
them to figure out

00:07:59.660 --> 00:08:01.743
why they've lapsed, and
then try to turn them back

00:08:01.743 --> 00:08:03.000
into customers.

00:08:03.000 --> 00:08:05.450
So it's really like an
organic state machine,

00:08:05.450 --> 00:08:08.210
and it's really important that
all the pieces fit together,

00:08:08.210 --> 00:08:14.210
because what you realize when
you start digging into this is,

00:08:14.210 --> 00:08:16.530
the majority of the
people go down this path,

00:08:16.530 --> 00:08:19.564
and then it's pretty easy
to cycle people around here,

00:08:19.564 --> 00:08:21.230
but if you're trying
to grow a business,

00:08:21.230 --> 00:08:23.960
really the difference between
having more regulars than you

00:08:23.960 --> 00:08:25.910
had yesterday is
really adding up

00:08:25.910 --> 00:08:28.050
all these other little
percentages that you can get

00:08:28.050 --> 00:08:29.300
throughout this entire funnel.

00:08:32.334 --> 00:08:33.750
So what are the
power of regulars?

00:08:33.750 --> 00:08:38.620
So in Marvel, over
90% of monthly revenue

00:08:38.620 --> 00:08:41.789
now is through regulars,
and we're still growing them

00:08:41.789 --> 00:08:42.960
three years into the game.

00:08:42.960 --> 00:08:45.470
So year over year,
we've continued

00:08:45.470 --> 00:08:47.220
to have our daily
number of regular users

00:08:47.220 --> 00:08:49.980
continue to rise, even
three years into the game.

00:08:49.980 --> 00:08:54.240
They end up having over a 97%
loyalty rate, which basically

00:08:54.240 --> 00:08:57.174
means month over month, they
are continually coming back.

00:08:57.174 --> 00:08:58.590
There's actually
months that we've

00:08:58.590 --> 00:09:01.140
had over 100% loyalty
rate, which is crazy.

00:09:01.140 --> 00:09:03.150
We've actually had cohorts
that are really old,

00:09:03.150 --> 00:09:05.934
that actually are still
continuing to grow regulars.

00:09:05.934 --> 00:09:07.350
And the beautiful
part of is, they

00:09:07.350 --> 00:09:09.990
have a really stable
ARPMARC, which

00:09:09.990 --> 00:09:13.680
is actually average revenue
per monthly active regular

00:09:13.680 --> 00:09:14.190
customer.

00:09:14.190 --> 00:09:14.940
[LAUGHTER]

00:09:14.940 --> 00:09:17.770
So, sorry about that.

00:09:17.770 --> 00:09:20.130
But ARPMARC, very
stable, which is great,

00:09:20.130 --> 00:09:22.770
because if you're looking
to forecast your business,

00:09:22.770 --> 00:09:25.380
you have a group of people
that never leave, and pay you

00:09:25.380 --> 00:09:26.610
the same amount every month.

00:09:26.610 --> 00:09:29.347
That's like, it's the
perfect forecasting tool.

00:09:29.347 --> 00:09:30.930
So when we view this
for our business,

00:09:30.930 --> 00:09:35.700
we really think about how we
can service these regulars,

00:09:35.700 --> 00:09:37.350
and we think about
this regularity lens

00:09:37.350 --> 00:09:39.420
throughout our entire business.

00:09:39.420 --> 00:09:41.550
And so we've applied
this throughout

00:09:41.550 --> 00:09:44.400
our entire operations.

00:09:44.400 --> 00:09:47.040
So how can technology help?

00:09:47.040 --> 00:09:49.170
So, I'm not going to
stress this any much,

00:09:49.170 --> 00:09:51.030
but you just, you
need a good BI system.

00:09:51.030 --> 00:09:56.100
Like data is the most imperative
part of this whole system.

00:09:56.100 --> 00:09:59.750
You need to have multiple
components of it,

00:09:59.750 --> 00:10:01.300
we can now walk through them.

00:10:01.300 --> 00:10:04.290
So the first one that we really
think is super-important,

00:10:04.290 --> 00:10:07.530
is we have this concept at Kabam
of the 360 view of the player.

00:10:07.530 --> 00:10:11.160
So a lot of game companies
are only viewing their world

00:10:11.160 --> 00:10:13.104
from what the player
does in the game,

00:10:13.104 --> 00:10:14.520
and that's not the
entire picture,

00:10:14.520 --> 00:10:17.610
Right So you have in-game
data, you have marketing data,

00:10:17.610 --> 00:10:19.920
you have data from your
community, maybe your forums,

00:10:19.920 --> 00:10:21.570
you have customer service.

00:10:21.570 --> 00:10:23.460
What you really want
to do is view all that,

00:10:23.460 --> 00:10:25.710
and get all that into
one spot, so you can

00:10:25.710 --> 00:10:29.690
see the 360 view of a player.

00:10:29.690 --> 00:10:32.700
At Kabam, like I said, we're
not go too deep in the deck

00:10:32.700 --> 00:10:37.140
but, BigQuery is sort of
that center hub of our data.

00:10:37.140 --> 00:10:40.890
We put a lot of
data into BigQuery.

00:10:40.890 --> 00:10:44.610
On Marvel, you're looking around
1 and 1/2 petabytes of data

00:10:44.610 --> 00:10:45.630
alone on there.

00:10:45.630 --> 00:10:47.610
It's about 2 trillion rows.

00:10:47.610 --> 00:10:50.160
We're ingesting three to
four billion events a day

00:10:50.160 --> 00:10:52.490
on just one single game.

00:10:52.490 --> 00:10:54.330
And the cool part is,
we've done table scans

00:10:54.330 --> 00:10:57.850
where you get a billion rows per
second on a query performance.

00:10:57.850 --> 00:11:01.410
So it's really quite impressive.

00:11:01.410 --> 00:11:03.870
Then we just hook
it up to Tableau.

00:11:03.870 --> 00:11:06.090
Great thing about Tableau
Online, it's sort of NoOps

00:11:06.090 --> 00:11:06.390
as well.

00:11:06.390 --> 00:11:07.620
Just like BigQuery,
you can hook it--

00:11:07.620 --> 00:11:09.036
it integrates right
into BigQuery,

00:11:09.036 --> 00:11:13.350
and we can build our
reporting on top of that.

00:11:13.350 --> 00:11:15.940
The last little piece
is, this is obviously,

00:11:15.940 --> 00:11:18.330
you know, the predictive--
predictive modeling

00:11:18.330 --> 00:11:20.050
is the thing these days.

00:11:20.050 --> 00:11:22.530
AIs, machine learning,
this is really

00:11:22.530 --> 00:11:25.849
about you have this great
huge dataset, learn off of it,

00:11:25.849 --> 00:11:28.140
train your models, and then
start predicting and making

00:11:28.140 --> 00:11:29.375
predictions about the game.

00:11:29.375 --> 00:11:30.750
And once you can
do that, you can

00:11:30.750 --> 00:11:33.600
do some just really neat stuff.

00:11:33.600 --> 00:11:35.805
So what we're going
to start talking about

00:11:35.805 --> 00:11:37.870
is sort of the
top of the funnel.

00:11:37.870 --> 00:11:39.480
So, people getting
into the game.

00:11:39.480 --> 00:11:43.320
How do you get more
regulars into the game?

00:11:43.320 --> 00:11:45.990
So, any game that
is successful is

00:11:45.990 --> 00:11:47.850
going to end up doing
performance marketing.

00:11:47.850 --> 00:11:51.090
So with performance marketing,
you're obviously buying ads

00:11:51.090 --> 00:11:52.620
on different channels
and platforms,

00:11:52.620 --> 00:11:54.120
they're coming into
the game, you're

00:11:54.120 --> 00:11:55.660
able to attribute to them.

00:11:55.660 --> 00:11:57.600
And then you can calculate
your yields, right.

00:11:57.600 --> 00:11:59.100
So if your yields
are really based off

00:11:59.100 --> 00:12:00.406
of how much money
you think you're

00:12:00.406 --> 00:12:02.531
going to get out of this
person versus how much you

00:12:02.531 --> 00:12:04.770
spent to acquire them.

00:12:04.770 --> 00:12:06.840
The real [INAUDIBLE]
about that is yields

00:12:06.840 --> 00:12:09.360
are very hard to calculate.

00:12:09.360 --> 00:12:13.560
So often people get fooled.

00:12:13.560 --> 00:12:15.370
Marvel is a really
interesting example,

00:12:15.370 --> 00:12:18.420
because what we
saw, since Marvel

00:12:18.420 --> 00:12:21.380
is a sort of, in some ways
it's a collection game, people,

00:12:21.380 --> 00:12:23.130
what we would see is,
we'd have, actually,

00:12:23.130 --> 00:12:26.377
these users that would
come into the game,

00:12:26.377 --> 00:12:27.960
and they'd spend
like crazy until they

00:12:27.960 --> 00:12:30.830
got all the champions that they
wanted, and then they'd leave.

00:12:30.830 --> 00:12:33.330
And just sort of like, if you
can think of it like a numbers

00:12:33.330 --> 00:12:35.330
point of view, you can
have a guy that comes in,

00:12:35.330 --> 00:12:38.170
spends $1,000 in a month and
leave, well that's actually

00:12:38.170 --> 00:12:39.300
not your highest LTV user.

00:12:39.300 --> 00:12:40.883
What you'd much
rather have is someone

00:12:40.883 --> 00:12:45.349
who spends $100 a month, and
does so for four or five years.

00:12:45.349 --> 00:12:47.140
That's the type of user
you want to get to.

00:12:47.140 --> 00:12:50.820
So if you just sort of optimize
all your ad spend based

00:12:50.820 --> 00:12:53.250
off day 1 to day 7
yields, you can often

00:12:53.250 --> 00:12:55.644
get fooled about actually
who your best users are.

00:12:55.644 --> 00:12:57.060
And you're actually
not optimizing

00:12:57.060 --> 00:13:00.986
well enough for your UA spend.

00:13:00.986 --> 00:13:02.610
So this kind of
brought to this concept

00:13:02.610 --> 00:13:04.560
that we have at Kabam
called auto-tune.

00:13:04.560 --> 00:13:07.260
So what we end up
doing at Kabam,

00:13:07.260 --> 00:13:09.630
is we have this system
called auto-tune,

00:13:09.630 --> 00:13:12.930
where we basically,
we buy tons of ads,

00:13:12.930 --> 00:13:15.807
and we spend money on them.

00:13:15.807 --> 00:13:18.390
And then, once they get in the
game, and we've attribute them,

00:13:18.390 --> 00:13:19.848
we have this machine
learning model

00:13:19.848 --> 00:13:21.810
that can detect regularity.

00:13:21.810 --> 00:13:23.310
And it can do it
really efficiently.

00:13:23.310 --> 00:13:26.555
In fact, it only takes about
eight hours of actual time,

00:13:26.555 --> 00:13:27.930
and we actually
know if someone's

00:13:27.930 --> 00:13:30.322
going to be a regular in
three months from there.

00:13:30.322 --> 00:13:32.530
And then what we do, is we
take that, we feed it back

00:13:32.530 --> 00:13:34.975
into the machine, and we
can calculate return on ad

00:13:34.975 --> 00:13:37.020
spend, we can calculate
LTVs based on this thing,

00:13:37.020 --> 00:13:40.101
since we know the retention,
we know the [INAUDIBLE] marks.

00:13:40.101 --> 00:13:41.600
And then we just
adjust our bidding.

00:13:41.600 --> 00:13:42.890
So if we're getting
tons of regulars here,

00:13:42.890 --> 00:13:44.140
we're going to throw
more money on here.

00:13:44.140 --> 00:13:46.139
If we get less, we're
going to try less on here.

00:13:46.139 --> 00:13:48.162
And then we can
say, we can optimize

00:13:48.162 --> 00:13:49.120
all kinds of campaigns.

00:13:49.120 --> 00:13:51.100
We end up having
thousands of campaigns

00:13:51.100 --> 00:13:52.810
and different creative
running at once,

00:13:52.810 --> 00:13:56.950
and then we just let
auto-tune optimize it for us.

00:13:56.950 --> 00:13:59.170
And like I was saying,
it's really, really tight.

00:13:59.170 --> 00:14:04.120
So this is a graph of the
difference in the score.

00:14:04.120 --> 00:14:08.952
So we're about 95%, 90% correct
on predicting whether or not

00:14:08.952 --> 00:14:11.410
somebody is going be a regular,
within eight hours, or not.

00:14:11.410 --> 00:14:15.730
So it's a really, really
useful model for us.

00:14:15.730 --> 00:14:19.072
And it works out
really quite well.

00:14:19.072 --> 00:14:20.530
So the next part
in the whole thing

00:14:20.530 --> 00:14:22.330
is sort of
strengthening players,

00:14:22.330 --> 00:14:26.890
which is really turning
players into regulars, so.

00:14:26.890 --> 00:14:29.030
Lots of books and
topics on this,

00:14:29.030 --> 00:14:30.891
but it's really about
habituation, right.

00:14:30.891 --> 00:14:32.890
Trying to make people
come back and do something

00:14:32.890 --> 00:14:36.780
every day, tons
of books on this.

00:14:36.780 --> 00:14:38.530
There's lots of different
ways to do that,

00:14:38.530 --> 00:14:40.090
and we can talk
about a few of them.

00:14:40.090 --> 00:14:42.820
So we believe at
Kabam, strongly,

00:14:42.820 --> 00:14:45.580
that social gameplay is
one of the strongest ways

00:14:45.580 --> 00:14:48.426
to get people to
play with each other.

00:14:48.426 --> 00:14:50.800
A lot of these games are like,
from a neurochemical point

00:14:50.800 --> 00:14:52.635
of view, are all
dopamine-type things.

00:14:52.635 --> 00:14:54.010
When you start
adding social, you

00:14:54.010 --> 00:14:55.759
start adding different
types of chemicals,

00:14:55.759 --> 00:14:57.820
different types of
feelings into the game,

00:14:57.820 --> 00:14:59.810
and you start adding
social pressure.

00:14:59.810 --> 00:15:02.650
And that's why games like
World of Warcraft and such

00:15:02.650 --> 00:15:03.880
have lasted for so long.

00:15:03.880 --> 00:15:05.830
It's because people come
there, and it's a social event,

00:15:05.830 --> 00:15:07.840
and people can still
play with their friends,

00:15:07.840 --> 00:15:09.950
and that's what they want to do.

00:15:09.950 --> 00:15:12.140
So from a technology
point of view, you know,

00:15:12.140 --> 00:15:14.770
people on the internet
aren't always the best.

00:15:14.770 --> 00:15:16.980
So when you start adding
chat and stuff like that,

00:15:16.980 --> 00:15:20.000
it's really important that you
have a good filtering systems.

00:15:20.000 --> 00:15:23.142
So as positive as a
experience can be,

00:15:23.142 --> 00:15:25.100
it can also be horribly
negative, too, as well.

00:15:25.100 --> 00:15:28.390
So it's really important that
you try to get that in there.

00:15:28.390 --> 00:15:31.390
Adding group chat is great,
and self-regulating chats,

00:15:31.390 --> 00:15:34.747
like alliance chats or stuff
like that, are also excellent.

00:15:34.747 --> 00:15:35.830
And then bridging the gap.

00:15:35.830 --> 00:15:38.110
If you can get real-time
chat translation there,

00:15:38.110 --> 00:15:39.520
you can start doing
some really interesting

00:15:39.520 --> 00:15:41.311
social interactions,
where you can actually

00:15:41.311 --> 00:15:44.670
use territorial enemies that
can communicate with each other

00:15:44.670 --> 00:15:45.940
to talk.

00:15:45.940 --> 00:15:47.361
It's really, really interesting.

00:15:56.210 --> 00:15:57.960
And then the next one
is really alliances.

00:15:57.960 --> 00:15:59.750
So alliances, at
least in our games,

00:15:59.750 --> 00:16:02.390
have just been
incredibly powerful.

00:16:02.390 --> 00:16:06.400
If you just take the
short-term retention metrics,

00:16:06.400 --> 00:16:09.350
the second someone gets in
alliances, their retention

00:16:09.350 --> 00:16:10.430
basically triples.

00:16:10.430 --> 00:16:13.820
So we always try to get
people into alliances.

00:16:13.820 --> 00:16:16.190
Finding the right type of
alliance for people and stuff

00:16:16.190 --> 00:16:18.189
is a very hard problem,
and is very interesting,

00:16:18.189 --> 00:16:20.960
and you can get some
great work on that.

00:16:20.960 --> 00:16:24.660
The social pressure
mechanism is really strong.

00:16:24.660 --> 00:16:28.310
So if you build events
around cooperation,

00:16:28.310 --> 00:16:30.140
or competitive loops
around getting people

00:16:30.140 --> 00:16:33.950
to do things together, you
can get these great engagement

00:16:33.950 --> 00:16:34.542
boosts.

00:16:34.542 --> 00:16:36.000
The funny thing
about this, though,

00:16:36.000 --> 00:16:37.700
is actually that also
the number one indicator

00:16:37.700 --> 00:16:40.130
of churn prediction is actually
when you get kicked out

00:16:40.130 --> 00:16:40.950
of your alliance.

00:16:40.950 --> 00:16:42.570
So people who get kicked
out of the alliance,

00:16:42.570 --> 00:16:45.050
we are on them right away to
get them into a new alliance

00:16:45.050 --> 00:16:46.820
as fast as possible, because
that's the number one

00:16:46.820 --> 00:16:48.111
predictor of churn in our game.

00:16:50.960 --> 00:16:54.360
And then the next one is kind of
what we call putting on a show.

00:16:54.360 --> 00:16:59.870
So we think, if you build a
game that someone can literally

00:16:59.870 --> 00:17:02.600
play for 24 hours a day,
you will probably never

00:17:02.600 --> 00:17:04.339
have any problem
monetizing them.

00:17:04.339 --> 00:17:06.760
You just want to keep them
in the game as long as time.

00:17:06.760 --> 00:17:08.450
We do not believe,
a lot of people

00:17:08.450 --> 00:17:10.839
believe in this sort of concept
of like [? corps loop ?]

00:17:10.839 --> 00:17:13.130
binging, and they can binge
too much, and they get fat,

00:17:13.130 --> 00:17:14.990
and then they, they don't
want to play the game anymore.

00:17:14.990 --> 00:17:17.500
Absolutely do not believe that,
have no data to prove that.

00:17:17.500 --> 00:17:20.000
People can, if you give them
time to play, and they play it,

00:17:20.000 --> 00:17:21.500
they will play it.

00:17:21.500 --> 00:17:25.760
Its really about giving
people stuff to do.

00:17:25.760 --> 00:17:28.130
So, if you kind of look
at what a week looks

00:17:28.130 --> 00:17:30.800
like in the Contest
of Champions,

00:17:30.800 --> 00:17:32.400
its all really about layering.

00:17:32.400 --> 00:17:38.450
So we have about 100 events
a week that are planned,

00:17:38.450 --> 00:17:39.960
and they all feed
into each other.

00:17:39.960 --> 00:17:42.339
So you want to build a system
that can kind of like--

00:17:42.339 --> 00:17:43.130
you can stack them.

00:17:43.130 --> 00:17:45.742
So you can do a couple of
these events that give you

00:17:45.742 --> 00:17:47.450
a rewards that go to
the next event, that

00:17:47.450 --> 00:17:49.280
go to the next event,
that work on there.

00:17:49.280 --> 00:17:50.390
There's different
types of events

00:17:50.390 --> 00:17:51.639
for different types of people.

00:17:51.639 --> 00:17:55.160
Some people play social
events, like the AVE/AVA.

00:17:55.160 --> 00:17:57.802
Other people play single
events like PVP and grinding.

00:17:57.802 --> 00:17:59.510
Giving all these
opportunities for people

00:17:59.510 --> 00:18:00.968
to play these
different experiences

00:18:00.968 --> 00:18:03.121
is really important.

00:18:03.121 --> 00:18:05.370
And what we've done is we've
done a massive investment

00:18:05.370 --> 00:18:07.490
in tools, it's our
back end called Sparx,

00:18:07.490 --> 00:18:10.040
so that we can basically
allow live game

00:18:10.040 --> 00:18:12.380
designers to tune the
game in such a way

00:18:12.380 --> 00:18:14.900
that this is feasible.

00:18:14.900 --> 00:18:19.230
And how we do it is largely
kind of four main areas.

00:18:19.230 --> 00:18:21.720
So the first one is
really about targeting.

00:18:21.720 --> 00:18:25.980
So having really strong
targeting capabilities

00:18:25.980 --> 00:18:27.650
in your back end is
extremely important,

00:18:27.650 --> 00:18:28.750
because what you can
do is, you obviously

00:18:28.750 --> 00:18:31.041
have to do split testing, so
you can make these changes

00:18:31.041 --> 00:18:32.090
and see how they happen.

00:18:32.090 --> 00:18:34.310
You can end up cohorting
players into different things,

00:18:34.310 --> 00:18:36.260
because someone who's
played the game for three

00:18:36.260 --> 00:18:38.020
years versus some guy who
comes in a different game,

00:18:38.020 --> 00:18:40.610
they're obviously going to
need to be cohorted in there.

00:18:40.610 --> 00:18:42.820
You can do geotargeting.

00:18:42.820 --> 00:18:46.340
Our back end has
but 200 dimensions

00:18:46.340 --> 00:18:50.311
and more, actually now, of
targeting that we can add to.

00:18:50.311 --> 00:18:52.310
And then you can also do
action-based targeting.

00:18:52.310 --> 00:18:53.893
So someone does
something, and then it

00:18:53.893 --> 00:18:57.710
changes how they handle it.

00:18:57.710 --> 00:19:00.680
The next real strong thing that
we really are quite proud of

00:19:00.680 --> 00:19:02.430
is this concept of
flexible economy.

00:19:02.430 --> 00:19:06.629
So all these games, frankly,
are kind of similar.

00:19:06.629 --> 00:19:09.170
They're all, where your money
is in all these games is having

00:19:09.170 --> 00:19:11.270
a real strong meta-game system.

00:19:11.270 --> 00:19:13.340
And a meta-game,
frankly, is really

00:19:13.340 --> 00:19:14.420
quite simple at its core.

00:19:14.420 --> 00:19:16.340
It's always you do
something, you do an action,

00:19:16.340 --> 00:19:17.256
and you get something.

00:19:17.256 --> 00:19:19.400
So you're always trading
one resource for another,

00:19:19.400 --> 00:19:21.191
and there's an action
in between the lines.

00:19:21.191 --> 00:19:23.060
So if you make that
generic enough,

00:19:23.060 --> 00:19:25.934
you can make your entire economy
generic in a way that is just,

00:19:25.934 --> 00:19:28.100
all you're doing is taking
this and doing something,

00:19:28.100 --> 00:19:30.014
and turning it into that.

00:19:30.014 --> 00:19:31.430
And if you build
tools around that

00:19:31.430 --> 00:19:35.420
so that your live ops can
actually make new items,

00:19:35.420 --> 00:19:38.450
and make new economies,
and shape them,

00:19:38.450 --> 00:19:41.357
then you can do some
really powerful things.

00:19:41.357 --> 00:19:43.190
And you can also measure
them, too, as well.

00:19:43.190 --> 00:19:46.150
So our abstracted economy
ends up building a, basically,

00:19:46.150 --> 00:19:48.440
an operation log for
every single action that

00:19:48.440 --> 00:19:49.830
has an economic value to it.

00:19:49.830 --> 00:19:53.281
So you have an oplog of every
single thing that happens,

00:19:53.281 --> 00:19:55.280
and you can measure that,
which allows you to do

00:19:55.280 --> 00:19:56.370
this cool stuff like this.

00:19:56.370 --> 00:19:59.150
So if you're looking at it like
a single currency in the game,

00:19:59.150 --> 00:20:01.940
you can actually see the flow of
currency and where it happens.

00:20:01.940 --> 00:20:03.770
So you can see in
the middle there,

00:20:03.770 --> 00:20:06.954
we saw this currency was getting
kind of high, so what we'll do

00:20:06.954 --> 00:20:08.870
is live ops and we'll
maybe make a sink, which

00:20:08.870 --> 00:20:11.647
might be like an event to
try to drain that currency,

00:20:11.647 --> 00:20:13.730
and that will give you a
different currency, which

00:20:13.730 --> 00:20:15.290
then they can make another
following event to drain it.

00:20:15.290 --> 00:20:18.020
So you can really kind of move
your economy around in ways

00:20:18.020 --> 00:20:20.060
that you can kind of
alter player behavior.

00:20:22.910 --> 00:20:24.530
And then the real
part about this

00:20:24.530 --> 00:20:29.180
is, anytime you get deep into
these data-driven analytics

00:20:29.180 --> 00:20:30.984
and data-driven
systems, you really

00:20:30.984 --> 00:20:32.150
need a way to manage it all.

00:20:32.150 --> 00:20:35.210
So, in the Contest
of Champions, there's

00:20:35.210 --> 00:20:37.130
about 200 to 300
megabytes of metadata

00:20:37.130 --> 00:20:39.030
that people are always
handling and managing,

00:20:39.030 --> 00:20:41.814
and you've got to build
tools around how you actually

00:20:41.814 --> 00:20:44.480
move the different pieces across
different environments, how you

00:20:44.480 --> 00:20:47.847
QA these, how you actually
merge them in and diff them,

00:20:47.847 --> 00:20:48.680
and stuff like that.

00:20:48.680 --> 00:20:53.510
And if you don't do that, you're
probably liable up for a mess.

00:20:53.510 --> 00:20:55.000
I remember one
time in old Kabam,

00:20:55.000 --> 00:20:56.050
before we had some
of these things,

00:20:56.050 --> 00:20:58.810
we had built a gotcha box once,
and we didn't have these tools,

00:20:58.810 --> 00:21:01.300
and it was a gotcha box that
opened 5,000 more boxes,

00:21:01.300 --> 00:21:03.180
and it was sort of
Inception, and it

00:21:03.180 --> 00:21:04.307
was a disaster to clean up.

00:21:04.307 --> 00:21:05.890
So I can't stress
this enough how well

00:21:05.890 --> 00:21:07.806
you need to have [INAUDIBLE]
about that stuff.

00:21:10.360 --> 00:21:13.120
And then the last one is really
about long-term retention.

00:21:13.120 --> 00:21:15.880
How do you retain
people for a long time?

00:21:15.880 --> 00:21:20.800
So it's really all about knowing
when someone's going to leave.

00:21:20.800 --> 00:21:22.360
You can pretty
much predict that.

00:21:22.360 --> 00:21:24.130
Like I said, there's some
really strong indicators.

00:21:24.130 --> 00:21:26.130
Getting kicked out of an
alliance is a huge one.

00:21:26.130 --> 00:21:28.390
Velocity changes, of
how much they play

00:21:28.390 --> 00:21:29.900
or how much they
engage the game,

00:21:29.900 --> 00:21:32.657
always yields pretty
accurate results.

00:21:32.657 --> 00:21:34.990
And then you kind of build,
you automate it then, right.

00:21:34.990 --> 00:21:37.019
So you have the
machine learning models

00:21:37.019 --> 00:21:39.310
that are running all the
time, generating fit scores on

00:21:39.310 --> 00:21:41.050
whether or not someone's
going to churn.

00:21:41.050 --> 00:21:43.180
If they end up going to churn,
then we'll start sending them

00:21:43.180 --> 00:21:44.740
what we think they need,
they might be missing.

00:21:44.740 --> 00:21:47.020
Maybe they'd been playing
in the game forever,

00:21:47.020 --> 00:21:49.580
and they just, they haven't
got the character they wanted,

00:21:49.580 --> 00:21:51.670
or they're running
out of currency.

00:21:51.670 --> 00:21:53.770
We're going to try to give
them a win-back prize.

00:21:53.770 --> 00:21:55.312
And if they don't
come, we'll take it

00:21:55.312 --> 00:21:57.228
to the next-level
automation, that we're going

00:21:57.228 --> 00:21:58.460
to send them an email, right.

00:21:58.460 --> 00:22:00.700
If they don't come after that,
we'll send them a push message.

00:22:00.700 --> 00:22:01.870
If they still don't
come after that,

00:22:01.870 --> 00:22:03.661
we can actually
automatically put them back

00:22:03.661 --> 00:22:06.981
into a UA campaign, and then
try to reactivate them that way.

00:22:06.981 --> 00:22:09.230
And then we can use deep
linking to actually give them

00:22:09.230 --> 00:22:10.540
reward through the UA campaign.

00:22:10.540 --> 00:22:12.760
So having that whole
automated system

00:22:12.760 --> 00:22:15.340
allows to claw back these
users that maybe left

00:22:15.340 --> 00:22:18.250
because of whatever reason, they
maybe didn't have something,

00:22:18.250 --> 00:22:20.200
could have been seasonality.

00:22:20.200 --> 00:22:22.542
I mean, we got Pokemon
Go'ed a few years ago,

00:22:22.542 --> 00:22:23.500
like everyone else did.

00:22:23.500 --> 00:22:25.520
And they, you know, kind of
came back to us afterwards.

00:22:25.520 --> 00:22:27.561
But we were able to claw
back a lot of the people

00:22:27.561 --> 00:22:30.740
during that time.

00:22:30.740 --> 00:22:32.180
And like I said,
it really works.

00:22:32.180 --> 00:22:35.680
I think we've raised, with this,
our daily revenues by about 3%

00:22:35.680 --> 00:22:39.624
to 5%, just with
the automated churn.

00:22:39.624 --> 00:22:41.290
Hardest part about a
lot of these things

00:22:41.290 --> 00:22:43.807
is trying to figure
out why they left.

00:22:43.807 --> 00:22:46.390
Deciding whether or not they're
going to leave is pretty easy,

00:22:46.390 --> 00:22:48.190
but if it's like
why, what do I need

00:22:48.190 --> 00:22:51.960
to give you to make
sure that you come back?

00:22:51.960 --> 00:22:53.560
And then with
re-activation campaign,

00:22:53.560 --> 00:22:55.665
often when you're
doing lots of UA,

00:22:55.665 --> 00:22:58.484
there is a certain
part, amount of spend,

00:22:58.484 --> 00:23:00.400
that it's actually way
cheaper to actually try

00:23:00.400 --> 00:23:03.350
to reactivate good users than
it is to go get brand new users.

00:23:06.370 --> 00:23:09.500
So in a conclusion here,
really, it's all about

00:23:09.500 --> 00:23:10.750
trying to avoid the shark fin.

00:23:13.270 --> 00:23:17.260
Games that can grow, and have
done this holistic approach,

00:23:17.260 --> 00:23:20.690
and use data to do that can
thrive for a very long time.

00:23:20.690 --> 00:23:22.390
I'm pretty confident
that Marvel is

00:23:22.390 --> 00:23:24.894
going to be a decade
game at this point.

00:23:24.894 --> 00:23:26.810
Like I said, we're going
into our fourth year.

00:23:26.810 --> 00:23:28.643
We're still continuing
to grow the business,

00:23:28.643 --> 00:23:30.790
and grow our number of regulars.

00:23:30.790 --> 00:23:35.650
And integrating the
technology around this really

00:23:35.650 --> 00:23:38.650
allows you just to kind of
handle the player's lifestyle.

00:23:38.650 --> 00:23:41.250
And like I said, every
single piece is important.

00:23:41.250 --> 00:23:44.087
If you don't do all the pieces,
you'll end up kind of like,

00:23:44.087 --> 00:23:46.420
everyday you're like-- it's
the difference between being

00:23:46.420 --> 00:23:50.680
like 99.9% percent of the people
you had yesterday, or 100.1%.

00:23:50.680 --> 00:23:53.120
It's just really that
close in a lot of cases.

00:23:53.120 --> 00:23:54.790
And just closing the
loop on everything

00:23:54.790 --> 00:23:57.997
is the way you do it.

00:23:57.997 --> 00:23:59.494
Thank You.

00:23:59.494 --> 00:24:02.487
[APPLAUSE]

00:24:02.987 --> 00:24:05.361
SPEAKER: Thanks so much, Jeff.

00:24:05.361 --> 00:24:06.236
Really appreciate it.

00:24:06.236 --> 00:24:08.152
Can we get another round
of applause for Jeff.

00:24:08.152 --> 00:24:09.460
He's [INAUDIBLE]

